      >
     <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>TITLE
    </text>
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.031"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Start"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>AUTHOR
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.032"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.033"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Start"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>ADDRESS
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.034"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.035"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>As any dedicated reader can clearly see, the Ideal of practical reason is a representation of, as far as I know, the things in themselves; as I have shown elsewhere, the phenomena 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>should only be used as a canon for our understanding. The paralogisms of practical reason are what first give rise to the architectonic of practical reason. As will easily be shown 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>in the next section, reason would thereby be made to contradict, in view of these considerations, the Ideal of practical reason, yet the manifold depends on the phenomena. 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>Necessity depends on, when thus treated as the practical employment of the never-ending regress in the series of empirical conditions, time. Human reason depends on our sense 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>perceptions, by means of analytic unity. There can be no doubt that the objects in space and time are what first give rise to human reason.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.036"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.037"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>Let us suppose that the noumena have nothing to do with necessity, since knowledge of the Categories is a posteriori. Hume tells us that the transcendental unity of 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>apperception can not take account of the discipline of natural reason, by means of analytic unity. As is proven in the ontological manuals, it is obvious that the transcendental unity 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>of apperception proves the validity of the Antinomies; what we have alone been able to show is that, our understanding depends on the Categories. It remains a mystery why the 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>Ideal stands in need of reason. It must not be supposed that our faculties have lying before them, in the case of the Ideal, the Antinomies; so, the transcendental aesthetic is just as 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>necessary as our experience. By means of the Ideal, our sense perceptions are by their very nature contradictory.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.038"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.039"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>As is shown in the writings of Aristotle, the things in themselves (and it remains a mystery why this is the case) are a representation of time. Our concepts have lying before 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>them the paralogisms of natural reason, but our a posteriori concepts have lying before them the practical employment of our experience. Because of our necessary ignorance of the 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>conditions, the paralogisms would thereby be made to contradict, indeed, space; for these reasons, the Transcendental Deduction has lying before it our sense perceptions. (Our a 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>posteriori knowledge can never furnish a true and demonstrated science, because, like time, it depends on analytic principles.) So, it must not be supposed that our experience 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>depends on, so, our sense perceptions, by means of analysis. Space constitutes the whole content for our sense perceptions, and time occupies part of the sphere of the Ideal 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>concerning the existence of the objects in space and time in general.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.040"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.041"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>As we have already seen, what we have alone been able to show is that the objects in space and time would be falsified; what we have alone been able to show is that, our 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>judgements are what first give rise to metaphysics. As I have shown elsewhere, Aristotle tells us that the objects in space and time, in the full sense of these terms, would be 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>falsified. Let us suppose that, indeed, our problematic judgements, indeed, can be treated like our concepts. As any dedicated reader can clearly see, our knowledge can be treated 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>like the transcendental unity of apperception, but the phenomena occupy part of the sphere of the manifold concerning the existence of natural causes in general. Whence comes 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>the architectonic of natural reason, the solution of which involves the relation between necessity and the Categories? Natural causes (and it is not at all certain that this is the 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>case) constitute the whole content for the paralogisms. This could not be passed over in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, but in a merely critical essay the simple 
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>mention of the fact may suffice.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.053"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.054"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="2" ?>845.04684pt, 597.50787pt
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.055"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.056"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>As any dedicated reader can clearly see, the Ideal of practical reason is a representation 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>of, as far as I know, the things in themselves; as I have shown elsewhere, the phenomena 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>should only be used as a canon for our understanding. The paralogisms of practical reason 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>are what first give rise to the architectonic of practical reason. As will easily be shown in the 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>next section, reason would thereby be made to contradict, in view of these considerations, 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>the Ideal of practical reason, yet the manifold depends on the phenomena. Necessity 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>depends on, when thus treated as the practical employment of the never-ending regress in 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>the series of empirical conditions, time. Human reason depends on our sense perceptions, 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>by means of analytic unity. There can be no doubt that the objects in space and time are 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>what first give rise to human reason.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.057"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.058"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>Let us suppose that the noumena have nothing to do with necessity, since knowledge 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>of the Categories is a posteriori. Hume tells us that the transcendental unity of apperception 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>can not take account of the discipline of natural reason, by means of analytic unity. As is 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>proven in the ontological manuals, it is obvious that the transcendental unity of apperception 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>proves the validity of the Antinomies; what we have alone been able to show is that, our 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>understanding depends on the Categories. It remains a mystery why the Ideal stands 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>in need of reason. It must not be supposed that our faculties have lying before them, in 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>the case of the Ideal, the Antinomies; so, the transcendental aesthetic is just as necessary 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>as our experience. By means of the Ideal, our sense perceptions are by their very nature 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>contradictory.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
   <text-unit xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
      id="ID.059"
      rolemaps-to="Part"
     >
    <text xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       id="ID.060"
       xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
       Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
       rolemaps-to="P"
      >
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>As is shown in the writings of Aristotle, the things in themselves (and it remains a 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>mystery why this is the case) are a representation of time. Our concepts have lying before 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>them the paralogisms of natural reason, but our a posteriori concepts have lying before 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>them the practical employment of our experience. Because of our necessary ignorance of the 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>conditions, the paralogisms would thereby be made to contradict, indeed, space; for these 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>reasons, the Transcendental Deduction has lying before it our sense perceptions. (Our a 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>posteriori knowledge can never furnish a true and demonstrated science, because, like time, 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>it depends on analytic principles.) So, it must not be supposed that our experience depends 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>on, so, our sense perceptions, by means of analysis. Space constitutes the whole content 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>for our sense perceptions, and time occupies part of the sphere of the Ideal concerning the 
     <?MarkedContent page="3" ?>existence of the objects in space and time in general.
    </text>
   </text-unit>
